LSX-64 Architecture Specification
A Loongson-Inspired 64-Bit RISC Processor Architecture
Version 1.0 — August 2026
  

1. Design Philosophy and Motivation
LSX-64 is an original 64-bit RISC processor architecture inspired by the design principles of LoongTech's LoongArch ISA and the broader Loongson processor family. It is not a LoongArch implementation, nor does it claim binary compatibility with any existing commercial ISA. Rather, it distills key architectural decisions from LoongArch — fixed-width encoding, a rich instruction format taxonomy, elimination of branch delay slots, PC-relative addressing, integrated SIMD/vector extensions, and binary-translation-friendly semantics — into a clean, modular, simulatable design.
1.1 Design Principles
Principle
	Description
	RISC Orthogonality
	All instructions are 32-bit fixed-width. Most instructions have exactly two source operands and one destination operand. Memory access is restricted to load/store instructions.
	No Delay Slots
	Following LoongArch's departure from MIPS tradition (LoongArch Reference Manual, Vol. 1), LSX-64 eliminates branch delay slots entirely. The instruction following a taken branch is not speculatively executed. This simplifies the pipeline and improves compiler code generation quality.
	Rich Instruction Formats
	LSX-64 defines 9 instruction formats (3 without immediate, 6 with immediate), inspired by LoongArch's format taxonomy (Linux Kernel Docs — LoongArch Introduction). This enables wider immediates (up to 24 bits in a single instruction) and larger branch offsets than classical MIPS.
	Modular Extensibility
	The base ISA (LSX-64I) is mandatory. Extensions for SIMD/vector operations (LSX-64V), binary translation assist (LSX-64BT), and atomic memory operations (LSX-64A) are optional and independently implementable.
	Principle Adherence
	The architecture adheres to classical RISC design principles: load-store purity, fixed-width decode simplicity, register orthogonality, and single-cycle execution for most ALU operations.
	

